<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cenwalh of Wessex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/672">672</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cenwalh was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> from about 642 to 672. He repudiated his wife, a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a>, and was driven into exile by Penda, spending about three years at the court of East Anglia, where he was baptised. After his return he extended West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rule westwards and is credited with founding the Old Minster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> calls Cenwalh the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynegils">Cynegils</a>, whom Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birinus">Birinus</a> baptised, and a great-great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a>, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> offers several ancestries for Cynegils and the family's links to later kings are uncertain. His name is of British rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> origin. Unlike his father, Bede writes, he at first refused the Christian faith, and when he put away his wife, a sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a>, Penda drove him from his kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The dates of his exile are unclear. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> gives it as three years, and since his host, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-east-anglia">Anna of East Anglia</a>, was himself driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a> in 651, it cannot have begun much later than 648; if Saint Felix baptised him, as William of Malmesbury states, that happened by about 647. The break with Penda probably followed soon after Penda killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a>, protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynegils">Cynegils</a>'s line, at Maserfeld in 642. Barbara Yorke places Cenwalh's return in 648, D. P. Kirby his exile in the 650s.</p><p>Back in power, Cenwalh tired of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames">Dorchester-on-Thames</a>, the Frank Agilbert, whose language he did not understand, and set up a second see at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> under Wini, in the heart of the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. Mercian pressure lay behind the shift: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>'s son Wulfhere ravaged Ashdown about 661 and gave the Meon valley to Æthelwalh of the South Saxons. In 658, according to the Chronicle, Cenwalh defeated the Britons at Peonnum and drove them to the Parret, yet he is also said to have endowed the British monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherborne">Sherborne</a>. In 665-668 he quarrelled with Wini, who fled to Wulfhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle Cenwalh died in 672 and his widow, Seaxburh, held power for about a year. No descendants of his are known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, and it is uncertain whether he ruled alone or shared power with Cenberht, father of King Caedwalla. It has been suggested that he was the Coenwalh from whom the 9th-century Mercian kings Coenwulf and Ceolwulf claimed descent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cenwalh of Wessex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
